 Based on the given input data, here's a JavaScript function that determines the most relevant computer networking protocol:
```javascript
function determineProtocol(inputData) {
  let protocol;
  if (inputData.protocol) { // Check if protocol is already provided in inputData
    protocol = inputData.protocol;
  } else {
    if (inputData.sourceIP && inputData.destinationIP && inputData.port) { // Check for IP addresses and port number
      if (inputData.port === 80) { // Port 80 is used by HTTP
        protocol = "HTTP";
      } else if (inputData.sourceIP && inputData.destinationIP && inputData.payload) { // Check for IP addresses and payload
        protocol = "UDP"; // UDP is generally used for datagrams with a payload
      } else { // Neither IP addresses nor port number nor payload is provided, assume IPv6
        protocol = "IPv6";
      }
    }
  }
  switch (protocol) {
    case "HTTP":
      console.log("The most relevant protocol is HTTP.");
      break;
    case "UDP":
      console.log("The most relevant protocol is UDP.");
      break;
    case "IPv6":
      console.log("The most relevant protocol is IPv6.");
      break;
    default:
      console.log("Invalid protocol provided.");
  }
  return protocol;
}
// Test the function with the provided input data
determineProtocol(inputData);
```
This function first checks if the protocol is already provided in the input data. If not, it analyzes the given data to determine the most likely protocol based on the presence of an IP address, port number, and payload. The function then returns the most relevant protocol as its output. In this case, since we have IPv6 addresses, the function will return "IPv6" as the most relevant protocol.

