 Sure thing! Here's a PHP function called `smart_sort_array` that meets your requirements:

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order = 'ASC') {
    $size = count($set);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $size - 1; $i++) {
        for ($j = 0; $j < $size - $i - 1; $j++) {
            if (($order == 'ASC' && $set[$j]['age'] > $set[$j+1]['age']) || ($order == 'DESC' && $set[$j]['age'] < $set[$j+1]['age'])) {
                $temp = $set[$j];
                $set[$j] = $set[$j+1];
                $set[$j+1] = $temp;
            }
        }
    }
    return $set;
}
```

This function takes an associative array called `$set` as its first argument, and an optional second argument `$order` with a default value of 'ASC' for ascending order. It uses a nested loop to compare adjacent elements in the array based on their 'age' keys, swapping them if they are in the wrong order according to the given sorting direction. The function returns the sorted array after processing.

