 To retrieve the username from a JSON Web Token (JWT) in an ASP.NET C# WebApi project, follow these steps:

1. Install the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` NuGet package if you haven't already. This package includes the `JwtSecurityTokenHandler` class which will help us parse the JWT token.

```sh
Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
```

2. Create a method to extract the username from the JWT token.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Claims;

public static string GetUsernameFromJwt(string jwt)
{
    try
    {
        // Parse the JWT token.
        JwtSecurityTokenHandler tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
        JwtSecurityToken jsonToken = tokenHandler.ReadJwt(jwt);

        // Get the username from the first claim.
        ClaimsIdentity claimsIdentity = jsonToken.Claims.ToClaimsPrincipal().Identities.First();
        return claimsIdentity.Name;
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        throw new Exception("Invalid JWT token.", ex);
    }
}
```

3. Use this method in your controller action or middleware to authenticate and retrieve the username from the JWT token.

```csharp
[HttpGet]
public ActionResult<string> Get()
{
    string jwt = Request.Headers["Authorization"].Value.Split(' ').Last(); // Assuming the JWT token is sent as a Bearer token in the Authorization header.

    try
    {
        string username = GetUsernameFromJwt(jwt);
        return Ok(username);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        return Unauthorized(ex.Message);
    }
}
```

This method retrieves the username from the first claim in the JWT token and returns it as part of the API response. Make sure that the user's identity is correctly represented by a single claim with the key "name" or "sub" containing their username. If not, update your authentication middleware

