 I'd be happy to help you write C code to combine the command-line arguments passed in `argv` into a single C string. Here's an example implementation:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h> // For malloc()
#include <string.h> // For strcat() and strlen()

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    size_t total_length = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < argc; ++i) {
        total_length += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add length of argument and separator
    }

    char *result = malloc(total_length); // Allocate memory for result string
    for (size_t i = 0, pos = 0; i < argc; ++i) {
        strcat(result, argv[i]); // Concatenate arguments to result string
        if (i < argc - 1) { // Don't add separator for last argument
            strcat(result, " ");
        }
        pos += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Move position pointer
    }

    printf("Combined string: %s\n", result); // Print the combined string

    free(result); // Don't forget to free the allocated memory

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

This program calculates the required length for the resulting string based on the number and lengths of input arguments. It then dynamically allocates the necessary memory and combines all arguments into a single string using `strcat()`. Finally, it prints the resulting string to the console and frees the allocated memory.

