 To prevent the planets from being generated too close to each other, you can implement a more efficient collision detection algorithm such as bounding sphere or axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) checking. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function using AABB checking:

```typescript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = []
  let attempts = 0

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    }

    let tooClose = false

    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const currentPlanet = planets[i]
      const distanceSquared =
        Math.pow(planet.position[0] - currentPlanet.position[0], 2) +
        Math.pow(planet.position[1] - currentPlanet.position[1], 2) +
        Math.pow(planet.position[2] - currentPlanet.position[2], 2)

      if (distanceSquared < Math.pow(minDistance + planet.size / 2 + currentPlanet.size / 2, 2)) {
        tooClose = true
        break
      }
    }

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet)
    }
  }

  return planets
}
```

This implementation uses a nested loop where we calculate the squared Euclidean distance between the new planet and all existing ones. If the distance is less than the sum of their radii (half of their sizes), they collide, and the new planet is not added to the list. This way, we ensure that the generated

 I'm an AI language model and cannot directly write or use React utilities, but I can certainly help you create one. Widows in typography refer to the last line of a paragraph that is incomplete due to being separated from the rest of the text by a page break or column break. Here's a simple React utility component to prevent widows in a given paragraph:

```javascript
import React, { PropsWithChildren, FC } from 'react';

interface Props {}

const PreventWidow: FC<PropsWithChildren<Props>> = ({ children }) => {
  const text = String(children);
  const lines = text.split(/(?=[\.!?])|(\s+)/g); // Split at sentence boundaries or whitespace

  const lastLineWidth = Math.max(...lines.map((line) => line.length * 12)); // Assuming 12px as the width of each character
  const maxWidth = 600; // Maximum width of the container

  if (lastLineWidth > maxWidth) {
    const extraSpaces = lastLineWidth - maxWidth;
    const newLines = lines.slice(0, -1).concat(['']);
    const shortenedLastSentence = newLines[newLines.length - 1].slice(0, -extraSpaces);
    const newParagraph = newLines.join(' ') + ' ' + shortenedLastSentence;

    return <p dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: newParagraph }} />;
  }

  return <p>{children}</p>;
};

export default PreventWidow;

// Usage:
<PreventWidow>Your text here...</PreventWidow>
```

This `PreventWidow` component checks the length of the last line of the text and adds extra spaces if needed to prevent it from becoming a widow. The component uses a simple heuristic to calculate the number of extra spaces based on the maximum allowed width. This utility component can be used with any text content inside the `<PreventWidow>` tag.

